## Lessons Learned

This lab reinforced the complete LVM storage hierarchy and demonstrated how Physical Volumes, Volume Groups, Physical Extents, Logical Volumes, filesystems, and mount points relate to one another.

The lab also demonstrated the importance of Physical Extent size. Because the Volume Group used an 8 MB PE size, Logical Volumes must be allocated in whole 8 MB increments.

This meant that 120 MB could be represented exactly:

```text
15 × 8 MB = 120 MB
```

but 100 MB could not:

```text
12.5 × 8 MB = 100 MB
```

Therefore, 13 PE were required for `lv300`, resulting in a 104 MB Logical Volume.

The lab also reinforced the importance of verifying failed operations. When XFS creation failed, `lsblk -f` was used to confirm that `lv300` remained without a filesystem.

The XFS minimum-size restriction was an environmental limitation that conflicted with the lab's requested 100 MB XFS Logical Volume. The available disk did not contain enough additional space to increase the LV beyond the reported 300 MB minimum while preserving the rest of the lab configuration.

---

## Real-World Application

LVM is widely used by Linux administrators because it provides flexible storage management.

Logical Volumes can be created independently of physical disk partitions and can later be extended when additional storage is required.

Administrators must understand Physical Extent allocation because the extent size determines the granularity at which LVM can allocate storage.

Filesystem requirements must also be considered when planning Logical Volume sizes. Different filesystem implementations can have different minimum or maximum size requirements.

Using `/etc/fstab` allows filesystems to be automatically mounted during system startup, while using device paths or UUIDs provides persistent filesystem identification.

